    "id": "p7-0415",
    "part": 7,
    "type": "RC",
    "question": "Why is the irrigation schedule changing?",
    "passage": "MEMO\n\nTo: All Greenhouse and Field Staff, Solara Growers\nFrom: Pieter Janssen, Irrigation Systems Manager\nDate: July 21\nSubject: Water restrictions and revised irrigation schedule\n\nThe Brackenfield Water Authority has placed our district under Stage 2 drought restrictions effective July 28. For agricultural users, this means a 20 percent reduction in metered water use compared with last July, with penalties of up to $5,000 for exceeding the allowance. The following measures will keep us within our limit while protecting the crops that matter most.\n\nThe drip lines in tomato houses 1 through 4 will switch to night operation, running from 10:00 P.M. to 4:00 A.M., when evaporation is lowest. Overhead sprinklers in the outdoor lettuce beds will run on alternating days only; field staff should check soil moisture probes each morning and report readings below 30 percent to their team lead rather than watering by hand, since hand watering is hard to meter and counts against our allowance.\n\nThe seedling nursery is exempt from the reductions, as young plants cannot tolerate water stress; its usage will be offset by deeper cuts elsewhere, primarily by pausing irrigation of the ornamental beds near the visitor entrance for the duration of the restrictions.\n\nMaintenance staff will inspect all drip lines for leaks this week; last summer, a single cracked junction wasted an estimated 9,000 liters before it was found. Anyone noticing wet patches, hissing sounds, or unusually low line pressure should report them to me immediately at extension 240.\n\nThese measures will be reviewed if the Authority changes the restriction level.",
    "options": [
      "New equipment is being installed.",
      "The water authority has imposed drought restrictions.",
      "The company is reducing its utility budget.",
      "Several drip lines are being replaced."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
