    "passage": "Security Office Chat — Fairmount Business Park\n\nDesmond Okafor (11:20 P.M.)\nNight shift update: the motion sensor on the Building D loading dock has triggered four false alarms since 9:00. Each time, the camera shows nothing but the new banner the tenants hung this afternoon flapping in the wind.\n\nKarolina Nowak (11:24 P.M.)\nSame thing happened with the flag at Building A last winter. Can you adjust the sensor's sensitivity remotely?\n\nDesmond Okafor (11:27 P.M.)\nNot for that model — it needs a physical adjustment at the unit. And per protocol, every trigger still requires an in-person check, which takes me away from the main gate for fifteen minutes each time.\n\nKarolina Nowak (11:30 P.M.)\nFour times a night is unsustainable. Options: ask the tenant to take the banner down, or get maintenance out to recalibrate tonight.\n\nDesmond Okafor (11:33 P.M.)\nMaintenance after hours bills the management company double time. A banner can come down in five minutes.\n\nKarolina Nowak (11:36 P.M.)\nAgreed, but the tenant's office is closed until 8:00 A.M. For tonight, I'll authorize switching dock coverage to camera-only monitoring, with the sensor logged as \"known fault.\" That keeps you at the gate.\n\nDesmond Okafor (11:39 P.M.)\nUnderstood. I'll note each flutter in the log but skip the walk-overs unless the camera shows something real.\n\nKarolina Nowak (11:41 P.M.)\nExactly. I'll e-mail the tenant first thing and ask them to mount the banner flat against the wall instead. Day shift gets a full handover note from me.",
