    "question": "What did the Riverton City Council recently do?",
    "passage": "RIVERTON (March 21) — The Riverton City Council voted unanimously on Thursday to approve a $48 million renovation of the Aldermere Bridge, the city's main crossing over the Penn River. — [1] —. Construction is scheduled to begin in June and will take approximately two years to complete.\n\nThe 70-year-old bridge carries an estimated 30,000 vehicles per day, and engineers have warned for nearly a decade that its support structures need extensive repairs. — [2] —. The renovation will also add a protected cycling lane and wider walkways on both sides, features that residents requested during a series of public meetings last fall.\n\nDuring construction, two of the bridge's four traffic lanes will remain open at all times. — [3] —. City transportation director Elaine Mosby acknowledged that delays are likely during peak hours and encouraged commuters to use the Garfield Tunnel or public transit whenever possible.\n\nFunding for the project comes from a combination of municipal bonds and a national infrastructure grant awarded to Riverton in January. — [4] —. Mayor Hugo Castellanos called the grant \"a once-in-a-generation opportunity to modernize a structure that every resident depends on.\"",
