    "type": "RC",
    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "CAPE MIRELLE (August 30) — Tourism officials in Cape Mirelle are celebrating a record-breaking summer. According to figures released Monday by the Coastal Tourism Board, the seaside town welcomed 412,000 visitors between June and August, a 28 percent increase over the same period last year.\n\nBoard director Lucia Ferreira attributed the surge to several factors, including the launch of a direct rail service from the capital in May and an aggressive social media campaign featuring the town's restored nineteenth-century lighthouse. \"For years, travelers viewed Cape Mirelle as a place to pass through on the way to larger resorts,\" Ms. Ferreira said. \"That perception has clearly shifted.\"\n\nLocal businesses have felt the impact. Hotel occupancy averaged 94 percent in July, and several restaurant owners reported their busiest season ever. Marina operator Joel Tran said bookings for his sunset sailing tours doubled, prompting him to purchase a second boat and hire three additional crew members.\n\nNot everyone is entirely pleased, however. Some residents have voiced concerns about crowded streets and limited parking. In response, the town council is considering a shuttle system that would carry visitors from a parking area on the edge of town to the waterfront. A feasibility study is expected to be completed by November.",
