    "question": "What is the goal of the Urban Canopy Initiative?",
    "passage": "GREENHOLLOW (April 17) — The city of Greenhollow officially launched its Urban Canopy Initiative on Saturday, an ambitious program that aims to plant 50,000 trees along streets, in parks, and on school grounds over the next five years. — [1] —. More than 300 volunteers attended the kickoff event at Pemberton Park, where the first 200 saplings were placed in the ground.\n\nCity environmental coordinator Naomi Okafor explained that the program targets neighborhoods with the least tree cover first. — [2] —. \"Mature trees lower summer temperatures, absorb stormwater, and improve air quality,\" Ms. Okafor said. \"The benefits are greatest in areas that currently have almost no shade.\"\n\nThe initiative is funded through a partnership between the city and Halverson Energy, which pledged $2 million over the life of the program. — [3] —. Residents can also participate by requesting a free tree for the strip of public land in front of their homes through the city's website. Each request is reviewed by an arborist, who selects a species suited to the location.\n\nSimilar programs in other cities have faced challenges keeping young trees alive. — [4] —. To address this, Greenhollow has hired four full-time watering crews and will ask neighborhood associations to monitor the saplings during their first three summers.",
