    "question": "What recently happened to Savoria?",
    "passage": "WESTBOURNE (November 12) — Savoria, a family-owned restaurant on Hartley Street, has been named Restaurant of the Year by the National Culinary Guild, the organization announced at its annual gala on Saturday. The award, considered one of the most prestigious honors in the hospitality industry, recognizes excellence in cooking, service, and community involvement.\n\nSavoria opened nine years ago when chef Antonia Reyes converted a former hardware store into a 40-seat dining room. Her menu, which changes monthly, draws on recipes from her grandmother's village and features vegetables grown on a cooperative farm just outside Westbourne. The guild's judges praised the restaurant's \"remarkable consistency\" and singled out its apprenticeship program, which has trained more than twenty young cooks from the local technical college.\n\n\"This award belongs to my whole team,\" Ms. Reyes said in her acceptance speech. \"Half of the people in our kitchen started as apprentices, and watching them grow has been the greatest reward of my career.\"\n\nThe recognition is expected to draw diners from well beyond the region. After last year's winner, a bistro in Calder Bay, received the award, its reservation waiting list reportedly grew to three months. Ms. Reyes said Savoria will add Sunday dinner service in January to accommodate increased demand but has no plans to expand the dining room itself.",
