    "question": "What is the main topic of the article?",
    "passage": "KESTREL FALLS (October 3) — Manufacturing activity in the Kestrel Falls metropolitan area grew at its fastest pace in six years during the third quarter, according to a report published Wednesday by the Lindqvist Economic Institute. Factory output rose 7.2 percent compared with the same quarter last year, and area manufacturers added an estimated 1,800 jobs.\n\nThe report credits much of the growth to the furniture and medical equipment sectors. Orders for office furniture have climbed steadily as companies across the country refurbish workspaces, while demand for diagnostic devices remains strong among hospitals replacing older equipment. The institute also noted that the opening of the Telford Intermodal Terminal in February has lowered shipping costs for local producers by giving them direct rail access to coastal ports.\n\nNot all indicators were positive. Several factory managers surveyed for the report said that finding skilled machinists remains difficult, and nearly a third reported turning down orders because they lacked sufficient staff. Wages for production workers in the region rose 5.1 percent over the past year, outpacing the national average.\n\nInstitute economist Hannah Brodsky cautioned that the pace of growth may moderate in the coming months. \"Borrowing costs are still elevated, and some of the current surge reflects customers placing orders early ahead of expected price increases,\" she wrote.",
