    "question": "What is the main topic of the article?",
    "passage": "CALVERTON (January 27) — Meridian Hospitality Group, one of the country's largest hotel operators, has agreed to acquire the Juniper Inns chain for $640 million, the companies announced Monday. The deal adds Juniper's 22 boutique properties, known for their restored historic buildings and locally inspired interiors, to Meridian's portfolio of more than 180 hotels.\n\nMeridian chief executive Walter Obi said Juniper will continue to operate under its own name and that its president, Celia Marchetti, will remain in charge of the brand. \"Juniper has built something special: small hotels with strong personalities that guests return to year after year,\" Mr. Obi said. \"Our role is to give the brand the resources to grow without changing what makes it distinctive.\"\n\nUnder the agreement, Meridian will fund the opening of at least eight new Juniper properties over the next four years, beginning with locations in Calverton and Port Esme that are already under development. Juniper guests will also gain access to Meridian's loyalty program, which has 14 million members.\n\nAnalysts say the acquisition reflects a broader trend of large hotel companies purchasing smaller brands rather than building new ones. \"Developing a boutique brand from scratch takes a decade or more,\" said industry consultant Greta Lindholm. \"Buying an established name is faster, and Juniper was the most attractive target left in the segment.\"",
