    "passage": "ISLE OF THARROW (May 5) — A new high-speed ferry linking the mainland port of Dunmarsh with the Isle of Tharrow made its first crossing on Saturday, cutting travel time between the two points from ninety minutes to just thirty-five. — [1] —. The vessel, operated by Cobalt Line, will run six round trips daily during the summer season.\n\nIsland officials have campaigned for faster service for years, arguing that the slow crossing discouraged day visitors. — [2] —. \"People on the mainland love the idea of Tharrow, but few would spend three hours of their day on a boat,\" said tourism officer Rhea Callum. \"Now a day trip is genuinely practical.\"\n\nLocal businesses are preparing for an influx. The Tharrow Heritage Museum has extended its opening hours, and several cafés near the harbor have added outdoor seating. — [3] —. Bicycle rental shop owner Pieter Hansen said he has tripled his fleet in anticipation. \"Last summer I turned customers away nearly every weekend,\" he said.\n\nThe ferry's arrival has not been without concerns. Some residents worry that the island's narrow roads and single supermarket will struggle on peak days. — [4] —. Cobalt Line has agreed to publish daily passenger counts so that the island council can monitor the situation and, if necessary, ask the operator to adjust its schedule.",
