    "type": "RC",
    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "ASHFORD GREEN (December 2) — The Ashford Green Public Library reopened its doors on Saturday following a fourteen-month, $9.5 million renovation, and hundreds of residents lined up in the cold for a first look. The project, the building's first major update since it opened in 1972, added a children's wing, a recording studio, twelve private study rooms, and a community kitchen for cooking classes.\n\nLibrary director Margarethe Oduya said the renovation reflects how the role of libraries has changed. \"Borrowing books remains at our core, but people also come here to attend workshops, to look for jobs, and simply to be together,\" she said. \"The old building, with its fixed shelving and single meeting room, could not keep up.\"\n\nDuring construction, the library operated out of a temporary space in the Marlowe Shopping Plaza, where circulation dropped by nearly half. Ms. Oduya expects numbers to recover quickly; more than 800 residents signed up for new library cards during the opening weekend alone.\n\nThe renovation was funded primarily by a municipal bond approved by voters three years ago, supplemented by a $1.2 million donation from the estate of longtime resident Edwin Marsh. In recognition of the gift, the new children's wing bears Mr. Marsh's name. The library's regular schedule resumes Monday, with extended evening hours now offered on Tuesdays and Thursdays.",
