    "question": "What has Larkspur Apparel announced?",
    "passage": "FOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE\nLarkspur Apparel to Eliminate Plastic from Packaging by Next Year\n\nMIDDLEFORD (September 17) — Larkspur Apparel, the outdoor clothing brand sold in more than 900 stores nationwide, announced today that it will remove all single-use plastic from its product packaging by the end of next year. The company will replace plastic garment bags, hangtag fasteners, and shipping pillows with alternatives made from recycled paper and a compostable film derived from plant starch.\n\nThe transition, which Larkspur has been piloting at its Middleford distribution center since January, is expected to keep an estimated 60 tons of plastic out of landfills annually. \"Our customers hike, climb, and camp in the places this waste ends up,\" said sustainability director Imani Walcott. \"They have been asking us to do better, and frankly, they are right.\"\n\nLarkspur acknowledged that the new materials will cost roughly eight percent more than conventional packaging. The company stated that it will absorb the additional expense rather than raise retail prices. According to Ms. Walcott, the pilot program revealed an unexpected benefit: the paper-based shipping pillows are lighter than the plastic ones they replace, which has modestly reduced the company's freight charges.\n\nLarkspur will publish a progress report every six months on its website, where customers can also find instructions for composting the plant-based film at home. The company joins a growing list of apparel brands, including Tornquist & Co. and Fellbright, that have made similar pledges in the past two years.",
