    "question": "Why did Pluvia Systems receive an award?",
    "passage": "VANTAGE CITY (November 20) — Pluvia Systems, a four-year-old company based in a converted garage in the Old Harbor district, took the top prize at Thursday night's Regional Innovation Awards for its rooftop rainwater collection system. The award, presented annually by the Vantage Chamber of Commerce, includes a $100,000 grant and a year of free legal and accounting services.\n\nPluvia's system channels rainwater from a building's roof into compact filtration tanks, producing water suitable for irrigation, cooling systems, and laundry facilities. Unlike conventional collection systems, which require large underground cisterns, Pluvia's tanks are slim enough to mount on exterior walls, making the technology practical for older buildings with limited grounds. The company's installations at the Brennard Hotel and two apartment complexes have cut those buildings' municipal water use by an average of 38 percent.\n\nThe judging panel of twelve business leaders and university faculty evaluated 87 entries over three months. Panel chair Dr. Simone Achebe said Pluvia stood out for both its engineering and its business model, which leases the equipment rather than selling it. \"Building owners pay nothing up front, so adoption is far easier,\" Dr. Achebe noted.\n\nCofounder Elise Tanaka said the grant will fund the company's first production hire and a demonstration project at Vantage City Hall scheduled for the spring. \"Two years ago we could not get anyone to return our calls,\" Ms. Tanaka told the audience. \"Tonight changes that.\"",
