    "question": "What is the main purpose of the article?",
    "passage": "HOLLOWAY BAY (January 15) — Visitors to the Holloway Bay region spent a record $1.9 billion last year, according to the annual tourism economic report released Tuesday by the regional tourism office. The figure represents a 12 percent increase over the previous year and marks the third consecutive year of growth since the slump that followed the closure of the Bayfront Amusement Pier.\n\nThe report highlights a notable change in how visitors spend their money. While spending on accommodations rose a modest 4 percent, spending on dining, guided tours, and cultural attractions jumped 19 percent. Tourism office director Casper Lindgren said the shift reflects the region's deliberate strategy of promoting food trails, kayaking excursions, and the newly restored Holloway Lighthouse rather than relying on beach traffic alone.\n\n\"The average visitor now stays 3.4 nights, up from 2.6 five years ago,\" Mr. Lindgren said. \"Longer stays mean more meals eaten in our restaurants and more tickets purchased at our museums. That money reaches far more local pockets than hotel bills do.\"\n\nThe report estimates that tourism now supports 14,200 jobs in the region, roughly one in nine positions overall. Still, the document notes challenges ahead, including a shortage of seasonal workers and the need for more midpriced lodging. The tourism office will present the full findings at a public meeting at the Holloway Bay Convention Hall on January 28.",
