    "question": "What is the purpose of the rail project?",
    "passage": "EAST GRENFORD (April 24) — Work began Monday on a long-awaited upgrade of the Grenford Valley rail line, a project that will allow passenger trains to travel at up to 200 kilometers per hour between East Grenford and the capital. — [1] —. When the work is finished in roughly three years, the fastest trip between the two cities will take 68 minutes, down from the current 105.\n\nThe upgrade involves replacing 90 kilometers of track, electrifying the entire route, and rebuilding four stations along the way. — [2] —. The most complicated portion of the project is the Darnell Curve, a sharp bend where trains must currently slow to 60 kilometers per hour; engineers will reroute 7 kilometers of track through a new cutting to straighten the alignment.\n\nNational Rail has promised to keep trains running throughout construction, though on a reduced schedule. — [3] —. Weekend closures will be necessary about once a month, during which buses will carry passengers between the affected stations.\n\nBusiness groups in East Grenford have long argued that slow rail service holds back the local economy. — [4] —. A study by the Grenford Chamber of Commerce estimated that the faster connection could draw as many as 3,000 new residents to the area within five years of completion, as commuting to capital-based jobs becomes practical. Property analysts already report increased inquiries about homes near the East Grenford station.",
