    "type": "RC",
    "question": "What will happen after twenty minutes of inactivity?",
    "passage": "MEMO\n\nTo: All Design Staff, Forsythe & Lang Architects\nFrom: Miriam Castile, Director of Information Technology\nDate: July 8\nRe: Transition to Floating Software Licenses\n\nOn August 4, the firm will replace individually assigned licenses for our drafting and rendering software with a shared pool of floating licenses managed by a central server.\n\nUnder the current arrangement, each workstation holds a permanent license whether or not it is in use; an internal review found that, on a typical day, almost a third of our licenses sit idle. The floating system will let any staff member check out a license on demand and will release it automatically after twenty minutes of inactivity. The change reduces our annual software costs by an estimated $48,000 — savings that will fund the additional rendering licenses requested by the visualization team for over a year.\n\nA few practical notes. Save your work frequently: if your license is reclaimed during inactivity, unsaved changes are preserved locally but cannot be edited until a license becomes available again. Staff working at client sites or on construction sites should reserve an offline license through the IT portal at least one day in advance; offline checkouts last up to seven days. Project managers may request priority access for teams facing submission deadlines.\n\nA demonstration session will be held in the main conference room on July 24 at noon. Pizza will be provided.",
