    "id": "p7-1162",
    "part": 7,
    "type": "RC",
    "question": "How much of each manuscript are interns expected to read?",
    "passage": "MEMO\n\nTo: All Editorial Interns, Larkfield Books\nFrom: Simone Petrov, Managing Editor\nDate: June 23\nRe: Guidelines for Evaluating Unsolicited Manuscripts\n\nWelcome to the summer internship program. One of your core duties will be preparing reader's reports on unsolicited manuscripts, and the following guidelines will help you do this consistently.\n\nEach intern will be assigned eight manuscripts per week through the submissions database. Read the full synopsis and the first fifty pages of every assignment; you are not expected to read entire manuscripts unless you recommend one for further review. Reports are due in the database every Friday by 4:00 P.M. and should run between 300 and 500 words, covering plot summary, writing quality, target readership, and a clear recommendation.\n\nUse the three-tier recommendation scale: decline, hold for discussion, or advance to senior editor. Fewer than five percent of submissions are typically advanced, so do not hesitate to recommend declining — a thoughtful explanation is more valuable to us than a generous rating. However, never contact an author directly for any reason. All correspondence with writers is handled by the editorial assistants, and an intern's e-mail to an author, even a kind one, can create legal complications regarding implied acceptance.\n\nWe will meet as a group on alternate Wednesdays at 2:00 P.M. to discuss notable submissions. Bring one manuscript you found promising, or one you found instructive to decline, to each meeting.",
