    "id": "p7-1185",
    "part": 7,
    "type": "RC",
    "question": "What problem did manual grading cause?",
    "passage": "MEMO\n\nTo: Retail Store Managers, Velocity Wireless\nFrom: Dario Fuentes, Director of Retail Operations\nDate: August 8\nRe: Trade-In Program Changes Effective September 1\n\nOur device trade-in program is being revised on September 1 to address two persistent problems: inconsistent device grading between stores and a backlog at our refurbishment center.\n\nFirst, all stores will use the new InspectPoint diagnostic station to grade trade-in devices. The station runs a twelve-point automated check — screen condition, battery health, water damage indicators, and more — and assigns a grade in under four minutes. Manual grading will no longer be accepted; in our audit last quarter, manual assessments at different stores valued identical devices as much as $80 apart, leading to customer complaints and credit adjustments. Each store will receive its station and a half-day staff training during the week of August 18.\n\nSecond, customers will now receive their trade-in credit as soon as the InspectPoint grade is confirmed, rather than waiting for the refurbishment center to re-verify the device. Center re-verification will continue behind the scenes, but our data shows its grades matched store-level InspectPoint results in ninety-six percent of cases during the pilot, so holding customer credit for re-verification is no longer justified.\n\nDevices with cracked casings or non-original parts remain ineligible for instant credit and must still be sent for full evaluation. Post the updated eligibility chart, included with this memo, beside each register by August 29.",
    "options": [
      "Devices were lost during shipping.",
      "Identical devices received widely different values.",
      "Stores ran out of replacement parts.",
      "Customers waited weeks for appointments."
    ],
    "answer": 1,
