    "question": "What makes the new loan's rates lower than standard rates?",
    "passage": "MEMO\n\nTo: All Loan Officers and Branch Managers, Pellworth Community Bank\nFrom: Susannah Greer, Director of Community Lending\nDate: March 24\nRe: Launch of the Storefront Revival Loan Program\n\nOn April 14, Pellworth Community Bank will launch the Storefront Revival Loan, a new product supporting small businesses that renovate street-level commercial spaces in the Old Mill and Tannery Row districts. The program is funded in part by a development grant from the city, which allows us to offer rates 1.5 percentage points below our standard commercial renovation loans.\n\nKey terms: loans range from $20,000 to $150,000, with repayment periods up to ten years. Eligible expenses include facade restoration, accessibility improvements, signage, and interior renovations of customer-facing areas. Back-office improvements and inventory purchases are not eligible under the grant's terms, so applications mixing eligible and ineligible costs must itemize them separately.\n\nApplicants must hold a current business license, have operated for at least one year, and provide a contractor's estimate with their application. Because grant funds are capped, the program will accept applications only until allocated funds are exhausted — based on inquiry volume so far, we project this could occur within four months.\n\nA briefing for loan officers will be held on April 2 at the main office, repeated by videoconference on April 4. Marketing materials, including window posters for participating branches, will arrive by April 9. Direct program questions to my office; media inquiries must go to corporate communications.",
