    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "From Checkout Lines to Cooking Classes: Retail Reinvents Itself\n\nWalk into Hartwell & Finch, a department store in downtown Avondale, and you may find a pottery workshop in the homewares section or a wine-tasting event near the food hall. Stores that once relied solely on merchandise sales are increasingly using in-store experiences to draw customers who might otherwise shop online. According to a recent survey by the Avondale Retail Council, 64 percent of shoppers under 40 said they were more likely to visit a store that offered classes, demonstrations, or live events. Retail consultant Priya Raman says the strategy works because experiences cannot be downloaded or delivered. \"A scarf can be bought anywhere,\" she explained, \"but an afternoon learning to knit one is memorable.\" The shift requires investment, however. Hartwell & Finch spent nearly $2 million converting its third floor into an event space, and it now employs four full-time event coordinators. Store director Malcolm Reyes says the spending has paid off: foot traffic has risen 22 percent since the renovation, and sales in adjacent departments have grown as well.",
