    "passage": "Suburban Office Market Shows Signs of Recovery, Report Finds\n\nThe quarterly market report released Tuesday by Calloway Property Research paints a mixed picture of the Brackenford metropolitan area's commercial real estate sector. [1] Downtown office vacancy remained high at 21 percent, virtually unchanged from the previous quarter. [2] In contrast, suburban office parks recorded their strongest quarter in five years, with vacancy falling to 11 percent as companies sought smaller, more flexible spaces closer to where employees live. [3] Average asking rents in the suburbs rose 4.2 percent year over year, while downtown rents declined slightly. [4] The report attributes the shift to the continued popularity of hybrid work arrangements, which have reduced demand for large central headquarters. Lead analyst Marcus Obi cautioned, however, that downtown properties with modern amenities are still leasing well. \"Buildings with fitness centers, upgraded ventilation, and conference facilities are outperforming the market,\" he noted. \"It is the older, unrenovated towers that are struggling to find tenants.\" Calloway forecasts that overall vacancy will begin to decline by the middle of next year.",
