    "passage": "Delivery-Only Kitchens Transform the Restaurant Business\n\nOn a quiet industrial street in Pellworth, a single warehouse houses eleven different restaurants—at least according to the food delivery apps. Inside, cooks prepare burgers, noodle bowls, and breakfast sandwiches for brands that exist only online. These so-called ghost kitchens, which prepare food exclusively for delivery, have seen a surge in numbers over the past three years. Industry tracker MealMetrics estimates that the region now has more than 300 delivery-only brands, up from fewer than 70 in 2023. The appeal for operators is clear: without dining rooms, servers, or prime storefront locations, a new restaurant concept can launch for a fraction of the usual cost. Chef Antonio Reyes, who runs three delivery brands from one kitchen, says he can test a new menu in weeks rather than months. Critics, however, note that the model depends heavily on app commissions, which can consume up to 30 percent of each order. Some operators are responding by building their own ordering websites, hoping to keep more of the revenue and gather customer data themselves.",
