    "passage": "Construction Begins on Meridian Bay Wind Project\n\nPORT ELLISON (April 8) — Crews began installing the first foundations for the Meridian Bay Offshore Wind Project on Monday, marking a major milestone for the largest renewable energy development in the region's history. [1] The project, a joint venture between Helios Renewables and the state energy authority, will eventually include 64 turbines located 25 kilometers off the coast. [2] Once fully operational in 2029, the wind farm is expected to generate enough electricity to power approximately 450,000 homes. [3] The development has also brought economic benefits onshore. A former shipyard in Port Ellison has been converted into an assembly facility, creating about 320 jobs, and local colleges have launched training programs for turbine maintenance technicians. [4] Not everyone has welcomed the project. Some commercial fishing groups have raised concerns about access to traditional fishing grounds during construction. Helios has agreed to fund an independent monitoring program and to suspend certain construction activities during peak fishing seasons. Project director Lena Okafor said the first turbines should begin delivering power to the grid by late next year.",
