    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "Against the Odds, Independent Bookstores Multiply\n\nA decade ago, many publishing analysts predicted the end of the neighborhood bookstore. Instead, the opposite has happened: the National Booksellers Alliance reports that its membership has grown for seven consecutive years, reaching 2,840 stores—the highest number since 2008. The new generation of shops looks different from the past. Most carry a smaller, carefully chosen selection of titles rather than attempting to stock everything, and many supplement book sales with cafés, stationery, and ticketed author events. Marguerite Allen, who opened Fernwood Books in Calder Heights last year, says her store survives on community connection. \"Customers can find any book online, often cheaper,\" she admitted. \"What they cannot find online is a person who remembers what they read last month and knows what they should read next.\" Publishers have taken notice of the revival. Several major houses now send authors on tours exclusively to independent stores, and one publisher recently introduced a program offering independents higher discounts on debut novels. Analysts caution that rising commercial rents remain the greatest threat to the trend, particularly in large cities.",
