    "passage": "Rental Market Cools After Two Years of Record Growth\n\nApartment hunters in the Sarsfield metropolitan area are finally getting some relief. According to the spring housing report from Tollman Analytics, the average monthly rent for a one-bedroom apartment fell 1.8 percent over the past quarter, the first decline in nearly three years. The change follows a steep rise in rents that began in 2024, when the average one-bedroom price climbed almost 22 percent in a single year. Analysts attribute the cooling primarily to a wave of new construction: more than 9,000 apartment units were completed in the metro area last year, the most in its history, and another 6,500 are scheduled for completion by December. \"When tenants have choices, landlords have to compete,\" said Tollman senior economist Farah Hadid. The report notes that many property managers are now offering incentives that were rare two years ago, such as one month of free rent or waived parking fees. Renters should not expect dramatic drops, however. Hadid predicts that prices will remain roughly flat through next year, since the region continues to add jobs and attract new residents. Suburban rents, the report adds, have been slower to decline than those in the city center.",
