    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "From One Oven to Forty Shops: The Rise of Beatrice Okonkwo\n\nWhen customers line up outside any of the forty Honeywheat Bakery locations across the country, few know that the chain began with a single secondhand oven in a market stall. Beatrice Okonkwo, the company's founder and chief executive, started selling sourdough loaves at the Elmsworth weekend market fourteen years ago while working weekdays as an accountant. Her breads developed a loyal following, and within two years she had left her accounting job to open a permanent shop. Growth, she insists, was never the goal. \"I only opened a second location because the first one had a line out the door every morning,\" she recalled in a recent interview. Okonkwo is known for an unusual expansion rule: Honeywheat opens a new shop only after it has trained a head baker internally for at least eighteen months. The policy slows growth but, she argues, protects quality. The approach has attracted attention beyond the food world; last month, Okonkwo was invited to lecture on sustainable expansion at the Calder Business School. She is also piloting a program that donates unsold bread to community kitchens, now active in twelve cities.",
