    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "Local Farms Find a Seat at the Table\n\nAt Juniper House, one of Glenbrook's most popular restaurants, the menu changes almost weekly. The reason hangs on the dining room wall: a chalkboard listing the seven nearby farms that supplied that day's ingredients. [1] Juniper House is part of a growing movement among area restaurants to buy directly from local growers rather than through national distributors. [2] The Glenbrook Restaurant Association estimates that 38 of its 110 member restaurants now source at least a quarter of their ingredients from farms within 80 kilometers, up from just nine restaurants five years ago. [3] Chefs say the approach delivers fresher food and stronger menus, though it requires flexibility. \"If the spinach crop fails, we rewrite the menu that morning,\" said Juniper House head chef Tomás Ferreira. [4] The arrangement benefits farmers as well. Hollis Creek Farm, which once sold exclusively at weekend markets, now earns 60 percent of its revenue from restaurant contracts, allowing it to hire two full-time employees. To strengthen these connections, the restaurant association will launch a monthly matchmaking event this fall where chefs and growers can meet and negotiate seasonal agreements.",
