    "passage": "Foundry District Becomes a Magnet for Young Companies\n\nThree years ago, the old Foundry District on Halverton's east side was best known for its empty warehouses. Today it hosts 47 startup companies, a transformation driven largely by the Halverton Launch Collective, a nonprofit accelerator that opened there in 2024. The Collective offers young companies six months of free office space, weekly mentoring from experienced executives, and introductions to regional investors. In exchange, participating startups must commit to keeping their headquarters in Halverton for at least three years after completing the program. The model appears to be working. Of the 62 companies that have finished the program, 51 are still operating, and together they employ more than 900 people. Graduates include Saffron Analytics, a data firm that recently signed a lease for an entire warehouse floor, and Pellucid Health, which makes scheduling software for medical clinics. Collective director Amara Diallo says the program's strength is its selectivity; fewer than one in ten applicants is accepted, and preference goes to founders who already have a working product and at least one paying customer. Applications for the next group, which begins in January, are due by October 15.",
