    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "Here Today, Gone Next Month: The Pop-Up Economy\n\nThe storefront at 12 Cordova Lane has held a candle maker, a vintage clothing seller, and a hot sauce brand—all within the past year. [1] Each was a pop-up shop, a temporary store that operates for a few weeks before closing, and each was arranged by StoreFront Now, a local agency that matches short-term tenants with empty retail spaces. [2] Property owners, who once viewed temporary tenants as a last resort, have come to appreciate the model. An occupied storefront deters vandalism, generates some rent, and keeps a shopping street lively while owners search for permanent tenants. [3] For the businesses themselves, a pop-up offers a low-risk way to test a physical location. Marisol Vega, who sells handmade ceramics online, used a three-week pop-up in March to gauge in-person demand. The experiment exceeded her expectations, and she has since signed a two-year lease one block away. [4] The city has taken notice of the trend as well; last month, the licensing office introduced a simplified thirty-day retail permit that can be approved within five business days, replacing a process that previously took up to two months.",
