    "passage": "Harbor Ferries to Get First Major Upgrade in Twenty Years\n\nMORELAND BAY (Feb. 9) — The Moreland Bay Ferry Service will modernize its aging fleet and terminals under a $95 million plan approved by the regional transit board last week. The service, which carries about 18,000 passengers daily between five waterfront communities and the city center, has operated essentially unchanged since 2006, and several of its vessels are approaching the end of their service lives. Under the plan, four new hybrid-electric ferries will replace the six oldest diesel boats beginning in 2028. Although the new vessels are fewer in number, each carries nearly twice as many passengers, allowing the service to increase total capacity while cutting fuel consumption by an estimated 40 percent. Terminal improvements will include covered waiting areas, real-time arrival displays, and ramps that adjust automatically to tide levels, which will speed up boarding for passengers with bicycles, strollers, and wheelchairs. Commuter advocacy group Bay Riders United praised the investment but urged the board to also extend evening service hours, noting that the last ferry currently departs at 8:40 P.M. Board chair Vincent Osei said a study of expanded hours will be completed by the end of the year.",
