    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "Building on Patience: Theodore Lam's Long Game\n\nIn an industry known for quick profits, commercial developer Theodore Lam has built his reputation on waiting. The founder of Lamwood Properties, now one of the region's most respected development firms, is famous for holding sites for years until the surrounding neighborhood matures. His best-known project, the Calloway Mills complex, illustrates the approach. Lam acquired the abandoned textile factory in 2014, when the riverfront district had little foot traffic and most investors avoided the area. Rather than building immediately, he spent five years leasing portions of the property at low rates to artists and small workshops, which gradually drew visitors to the district. Only then did Lamwood begin its full renovation, converting the mills into a mix of offices, shops, and apartments that is now fully leased with a waiting list. \"Everyone wants to be the last investor into a neighborhood,\" Lam said in a rare interview. \"I would rather be the first, and then be patient.\" Lam, who grew up above his parents' hardware store, says the experience taught him that districts thrive when small businesses can afford to stay. Accordingly, every Lamwood project reserves 15 percent of its commercial space for local independent businesses at below-market rents.",
