    "question": "What is the article mainly about?",
    "passage": "Translated Fiction Finds a Wider Audience\n\nFor decades, novels translated from other languages occupied a quiet corner of the book trade, accounting for barely 3 percent of fiction sales. That corner is getting crowded. New figures from the Bookscan Literary Index show that sales of translated fiction rose 28 percent last year, the fourth straight year of double-digit growth, with readers under 35 driving most of the increase. Booksellers point to several causes. Literary prizes have raised the visibility of translators, who now frequently appear on book covers alongside authors. Social media reading communities have championed novels from Korea, Japan, and Latin America, sometimes propelling decade-old titles onto bestseller lists. And a wave of small publishing houses devoted to translation has widened the selection available in bookstores. Dorothea Quint, who founded the translation-focused press Meridian Editions nine years ago, says the change is generational. \"Younger readers simply do not regard a translated book as foreign,\" she said. \"They follow stories, not borders.\" Major publishers are responding: at least three large houses have launched translation imprints in the past two years, and advances paid for translation rights have reportedly tripled for sought-after titles. Quint welcomes the competition, saying it benefits translators most of all.",
