    "type": "RC",
    "question": "What does Ms. Okonjo suggest the magazine do?",
    "passage": "LOGISTICS MONTHLY — Industry Outlook\n\"Smaller Warehouses, Faster Cities\" by Han Bekele\nA growing number of retailers are abandoning the traditional model of one giant distribution center per region. Instead, they are leasing networks of small urban warehouses, often under 2,000 square meters, located within city limits. Proponents say these \"micro-hubs\" cut average delivery times from two days to under five hours and reduce fuel use because delivery vans travel shorter distances—some operators even use cargo bicycles for the final kilometer. Critics counter that urban rents make micro-hubs three to four times more expensive per square meter and that finding suitable buildings is difficult. Nevertheless, our survey of 80 logistics firms found that 62 percent plan to open at least one micro-hub within two years.\n\n--- Passage 2 ---\nLetters to the Editor\nDear Logistics Monthly,\nI read Mr. Bekele's article on micro-hubs with great interest, but I believe it overlooked a key obstacle. My company operates three urban warehouses in Selton, and our biggest challenge has never been rent—it is the shortage of overnight loading zones. City regulations prohibit trucks from restocking our hubs between 7 A.M. and 8 P.M., yet residential noise rules also restrict deliveries after 10 P.M. That leaves a very narrow window. Until municipal governments modernize these rules, the five-hour delivery promise mentioned in the article will be hard to keep. I would welcome a follow-up piece on how cities abroad have solved this.\nGrace Okonjo, Operations Director, Selton Express",
